  Many sayings are traditionally attributed to Solomon through the biblical books of Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, and Song of Songs. These writings mix wisdom, skepticism, poetry, governance, morality, and reflections on human nature.

Here are some of the most famous sayings associated with Solomon:

Wisdom & Understanding

“The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom.”

“Trust in the Lord with all your heart and lean not on your own understanding.”

“Wisdom is more precious than rubies.”

“As iron sharpens iron, so one person sharpens another.”

Speech & Silence

“A gentle answer turns away wrath, but a harsh word stirs up anger.”

“Even fools are thought wise if they keep silent.”

“Death and life are in the power of the tongue.”

“Too much talk leads to sin.”

Wealth & Vanity

From Ecclesiastes:

“Vanity of vanities; all is vanity.”

“To everything there is a season.”

“Whoever loves money never has enough.”

“The race is not to the swift, nor the battle to the strong.”

“Of making many books there is no end.”

Human Nature

“Pride goes before destruction.”

“There is nothing new under the sun.”

“A cheerful heart is good medicine.”

“Better a patient person than a warrior.”

“Better little with righteousness than much gain with injustice.”

Love & Beauty

From Song of Songs:

“Many waters cannot quench love.”

“I am my beloved’s and my beloved is mine.”

“Love is strong as death.”

Justice & Leadership

“By justice a king gives a country stability.”

“Where there is no vision, the people perish.”

“The righteous care about justice for the poor.”

Often-Quoted Solomon Themes

His writings repeatedly return to:

the limits of wealth
the fleeting nature of life
restraint in speech
patience over force
wisdom over power
humility before God
the mystery and unpredictability of existence

Ecclesiastes especially stands out because it sounds surprisingly modern — reflective, existential, and sometimes almost philosophical in a secular sense.





  
